ministerial code in this country is very  much  central to upholding standards in public  life the prime minister is expected to  adhere to it and enforce it and yet at  this moment on the week the sugree  report was released and the behavior of  those at the heart of government has  very much been called into question it's  been watered down now the changes will  mean that breaking the ministerial code  will no longer be considered an  automatic resigning matter and that  minor breaches can be dealt with in  different ways via a reduction in salary  or an apology for example and in  practice that is often  how the code has been uh applied the  problem for the prime minister is that  he is about to be investigated by the  privileges committee who will look into  whether he misled parliament  over the downing street parties now  these changes will not directly affect  that but unsurprisingly opposition mps  have jumped on this today and said that  this is him  changing the rules ahead of that inquiry  trying to get himself  off the hook the slightly broader  problem will be the perception of this  by the public but also  by his own mps many of whom are still  very unhappy about what came out in the  sugray report and are still deciding on  how they're going to react and we've  heard this week  some of those mps coming out publicly  and saying that they no longer have  confidence in him and that it's time for  him to go it would take 54 letters of no  confidence to trigger a vote of no  confidence in the prime minister and  there will undoubtedly be concerned in  downing street that if this drip drip of  letters continues that the prime  minister could very soon find himself in  real trouble
 

